WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.377
Coding Exercise 2 asks for one row per diagnosis with an admission count.

00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:08.777
The phrase one row per diagnosis tells you the GROUP BY grain.

00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:14.827
Add the aggregate count, group by diagnosis, and sort so the largest count appears first.

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:18.037
Use Run and Check when you are ready.

00:00:18.037 --> 00:00:22.922
After the query runs, inspect the first row.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:26.742
Ask whether the diagnosis with the largest number of admissions is at the top.

00:00:26.742 --> 00:00:35.032
If your result has one row per admission instead of one row per diagnosis, revisit the grouping.

00:00:35.032 --> 00:00:39.652
If PostgreSQL reports a grouping error, check whether every selected non-aggregate column is represented

00:00:39.652 --> 00:00:47.012
correctly. The goal is to connect the written requirement directly to the GROUP BY list.

00:00:47.012 --> 00:00:50.637
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:50.637 --> 00:00:55.692
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:55.692 --> 00:01:01.497
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:05.637
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:12.702
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:12.702 --> 00:01:16.877
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:16.877 --> 00:01:21.580
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
